@page "/guide"
@inject NavigationManager Nav
@inject IJSRuntime JS
@implements IDisposable

<PageTitle>Spanish tenses explained · Conjugar</PageTitle>

<article class="guide">
    <header class="guide-head">
        <h1>Spanish tenses explained</h1>
        <p class="lede">
            What each mood and tense is for, how the regular forms are built, and a few examples.
            Every table on a verb page links here.
        </p>
        <nav class="toc" aria-label="Contents">
            <a href="guide#basics">The basics</a>
            @foreach (var mood in Moods)
            {
                <a href="guide#@mood.Id">@mood.Name</a>
            }
            <a href="guide#formas-no-personales">Infinitive, gerund, participle</a>
            <a href="guide#pronominales">Reflexive verbs</a>
        </nav>
    </header>

    <section id="basics" class="guide-section">
        <h2>The basics</h2>
        <dl class="facts">
            <dt>Three conjugations</dt>
            <dd>
                Verbs are grouped by the ending of the infinitive: <em lang="es">-ar</em> (<em lang="es">hablar</em>),
                <em lang="es">-er</em> (<em lang="es">comer</em>) and <em lang="es">-ir</em> (<em lang="es">vivir</em>). Remove the
                ending to get the stem (<em lang="es">habl-</em>) and add the endings of the tense. <em lang="es">-er</em> and
                <em lang="es">-ir</em> verbs share most endings.
            </dd>

            <dt>Six persons, and <span lang="es">usted</span></dt>
            <dd>
                <em lang="es">yo</em> (I), <em lang="es">tú</em> (you), <em lang="es">él/ella</em> (he/she), <em lang="es">nosotros/as</em>
                (we), <em lang="es">vosotros/as</em> (you, plural) and <em lang="es">ellos/ellas</em> (they). The polite
                <em lang="es">usted</em> and <em lang="es">ustedes</em> take the third-person forms. In Latin America
                <em lang="es">ustedes</em> is the only plural "you", so the <em lang="es">vosotros</em> forms are used only in Spain.
                The ending already shows the person, so the pronoun is normally left out: <em lang="es">hablo</em> means "I speak".
            </dd>

            <dt><span lang="es">Vos</span></dt>
            <dd>
                In Argentina, Uruguay, Paraguay and much of Central America, <em lang="es">vos</em> replaces <em lang="es">tú</em>, with
                its own forms in the present, the present subjunctive and the imperative: <em lang="es">vos hablás, comés, vivís;
                ¡hablá!</em>. Tick <strong>vos &amp; regional forms</strong> on a verb page to see them.
            </dd>

            <dt>Simple and compound tenses</dt>
            <dd>
                Simple tenses are one word (<em lang="es">hablo, hablaba, hablaré</em>). Compound tenses combine <em lang="es">haber</em>
                with the past participle (<em lang="es">he hablado</em>). Spanish uses <em lang="es">haber</em> for every verb, and the
                participle never changes: <em lang="es">ella ha ido, ellas han ido</em>. Every simple tense has a compound partner: the
                auxiliary takes that simple tense.
            </dd>

            <dt>Stem-changing verbs</dt>
            <dd>
                Many verbs change the stem vowel when it is stressed: <em lang="es">e → ie</em> (<em lang="es">pensar → pienso</em>),
                <em lang="es">o → ue</em> (<em lang="es">poder → puedo</em>) and, in <em lang="es">-ir</em> verbs, <em lang="es">e → i</em>
                (<em lang="es">pedir → pido</em>). The <em lang="es">nosotros</em> and <em lang="es">vosotros</em> forms keep the plain
                stem (<em lang="es">pensamos</em>). The verb page shows the change in the badge: <em lang="es">-ar · e → ie</em>.
            </dd>

            <dt>Spelling changes that are still regular</dt>
            <dd>
                Some consonants change spelling to keep their sound: <em lang="es">c → qu, g → gu, z → c</em> before <em>e</em>
                (<em lang="es">busqué, pagué, empecé</em>) and <em lang="es">c → z, g → j</em> before <em>a</em> and <em>o</em>
                (<em lang="es">venzo, cojo</em>). An unstressed <em>i</em> between vowels becomes <em>y</em> (<em lang="es">leer → leyó,
                leyendo</em>), and some verbs write an accent on a stressed <em>i</em> or <em>u</em> (<em lang="es">enviar → envío,
                actuar → actúo</em>).
            </dd>

            <dt>What the red forms mean</dt>
            <dd>
                Conjugar builds the regular form for every slot and compares it with the real form. Forms that differ are shown in
                <span class="irr">red</span>. The spelling changes above don't count; stem changes do, so <em lang="es">pienso</em> is
                red. The comparison can also catch mistakes in the source data: a verb with no stem change whose forms are mostly red
                gets a warning.
            </dd>
        </dl>
    </section>

    @foreach (var mood in Moods)
    {
        <section id="@mood.Id" class="guide-section">
            <h2>@mood.Name <small>@mood.English</small></h2>
            <p class="mood-intro">@((MarkupString)mood.Intro)</p>

            @foreach (var t in mood.Tenses)
            {
                <div id="@t.Id" class="tense-guide @(t.Compound ? "compound" : "")">
                    <h3><span lang="es">@t.Name</span> <small>@t.English</small></h3>
                    <p><strong>Used for:</strong> @((MarkupString)t.Usage)</p>
                    <p><strong>Formed:</strong> @((MarkupString)t.Formation)</p>
                    @if (t.Endings is not null)
                    {
                        <table class="endings">
                            <thead>
                                <tr><th></th><th lang="es">hablar</th><th lang="es">comer</th><th lang="es">vivir</th></tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                @for (var p = 0; p < t.Endings.Length; p++)
                                {
                                    <tr>
                                        <th scope="row" lang="es">@t.Pronouns[p]</th>
                                        @foreach (var form in t.Endings[p])
                                        {
                                            <td lang="es">@Stem(form)<b>@Ending(form)</b></td>
                                        }
                                    </tr>
                                }
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    }
                    <ul class="examples">
                        @foreach (var (es, en) in t.Examples)
                        {
                            <li><span lang="es">@es</span> <span class="en">@en</span></li>
                        }
                    </ul>
                    @if (Links.TryGetValue(t.Id, out var verbs))
                    {
                        <p class="see">See it in: @foreach (var v in verbs) { <a href="verb/@v" lang="es">@v</a><span class="comma">, </span> }</p>
                    }
                </div>
            }
        </section>
    }

    <section id="formas-no-personales" class="guide-section">
        <h2>Infinitive, gerund and participle <small lang="es">formas no personales</small></h2>
        <p class="mood-intro">These forms don't change for the person.</p>

        <div id="infinitivo" class="tense-guide">
            <h3><span lang="es">infinitivo</span> <small>infinitive</small></h3>
            <p>
                <strong>Used for:</strong> the dictionary form; after modal verbs (<em lang="es">quiero, puedo, tengo que</em>), after
                prepositions, as a noun (<em lang="es">fumar es malo</em>), and in the near future <em lang="es">ir a</em> + infinitive
                (<em lang="es">voy a comer</em>). The <span id="infinitivo-compuesto"><em lang="es">infinitivo compuesto</em></span>
                (<em lang="es">haber hablado</em>) is something done before.
            </p>
            <ul class="examples">
                <li><span lang="es">Quiero aprender español.</span> <span class="en">I want to learn Spanish.</span></li>
                <li><span lang="es">Mañana voy a llamar a mi madre.</span> <span class="en">Tomorrow I'm going to call my mother.</span></li>
                <li><span lang="es">Gracias por haber venido.</span> <span class="en">Thank you for coming.</span></li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="gerundio" class="tense-guide">
            <h3><span lang="es">gerundio</span> <small>gerund</small></h3>
            <p>
                <strong>Used for:</strong> an action in progress with <em lang="es">estar</em> (<em lang="es">estoy leyendo</em> = I am
                reading), and how or while something happens. Endings: <em lang="es">-ando</em> for <em lang="es">-ar</em> verbs,
                <em lang="es">-iendo</em> for the others. The <span id="gerundio-compuesto"><em lang="es">gerundio compuesto</em></span>
                (<em lang="es">habiendo hablado</em>) means "having done".
            </p>
            <ul class="examples">
                <li><span lang="es">Estoy leyendo un libro.</span> <span class="en">I'm reading a book.</span></li>
                <li><span lang="es">Llegó corriendo.</span> <span class="en">He came running.</span></li>
                <li><span lang="es">Habiendo terminado el trabajo, se fue a casa.</span> <span class="en">Having finished work, he went home.</span></li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="participio" class="tense-guide">
            <h3><span lang="es">participio</span> <small>past participle</small></h3>
            <p>
                <strong>Used for:</strong> all compound tenses (where it never changes), the passive with <em lang="es">ser</em>, and
                as an adjective (where it agrees: <em lang="es">una puerta abierta</em>). Regular endings: <em lang="es">-ado</em> and
                <em lang="es">-ido</em> (<em lang="es">hablado, comido, vivido</em>). Some common verbs have an irregular one:
                <em lang="es">hecho, dicho, visto, puesto, escrito, abierto, vuelto, muerto, roto</em>.
            </p>
            <ul class="examples">
                <li><span lang="es">La carta fue escrita por Ana.</span> <span class="en">The letter was written by Ana.</span></li>
                <li><span lang="es">una puerta abierta</span> <span class="en">an open door</span></li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="pronominales" class="guide-section">
        <h2>Reflexive verbs <small lang="es">verbos pronominales</small></h2>
        <p>
            Reflexive verbs end in <em lang="es">-se</em> (<em lang="es">lavarse</em> = to wash oneself, <em lang="es">llamarse</em> =
            to be called). They use the pronouns <em lang="es">me, te, se, nos, os, se</em>. The pronoun goes before a conjugated verb
            (<em lang="es">me lavo, no te laves</em>) and is attached to the end of the infinitive (<em lang="es">lavarse</em>), the
            gerund (<em lang="es">lavándose</em>) and the positive imperative (<em lang="es">¡lávate! ¡lavémonos! ¡lavaos!</em>),
            which then often needs a written accent. <em lang="es">Nosotros</em> drops the final <em>s</em> and
            <em lang="es">vosotros</em> the final <em>d</em> before the pronoun. Compound tenses use <em lang="es">haber</em> as usual:
            <em lang="es">me he lavado</em>.
        </p>
        <p class="see">See it in: <a href="verb/lavarse" lang="es">lavarse</a>, <a href="verb/llamarse" lang="es">llamarse</a>, <a href="verb/despertarse" lang="es">despertarse</a></p>
    </section>
</article>

@code {
    private sealed record GuideTense(
        string Id, string Name, string English, bool Compound, string Usage, string Formation,
        string[] Pronouns, string[][]? Endings, (string Es, string En)[] Examples);

    private sealed record GuideMood(string Id, string Name, string English, string Intro, GuideTense[] Tenses);

    private static readonly string[] Yo = ["yo", "tú", "él/ella", "nosotros", "vosotros", "ellos/ellas"];
    private static readonly string[] Imp = ["(tú)", "(usted)", "(nosotros)", "(vosotros)", "(ustedes)"];

    // "habl|o" = stem + ending; the ending is shown in bold.
    private static string Stem(string f) => f.Split('|')[0];
    private static string Ending(string f) => f.Contains('|') ? f.Split('|')[1] : "";

    private static string[][] Rows(string[] ar, string[] er, string[] ir) =>
        ar.Select((_, i) => new[] { "habl|" + ar[i], "com|" + er[i], "viv|" + ir[i] }).ToArray();

    private static string[][] Whole(string[] ar, string[] er, string[] ir) =>
        ar.Select((_, i) => new[] { "hablar|" + ar[i], "comer|" + er[i], "vivir|" + ir[i] }).ToArray();

    private const string CompoundNote = "Every compound tense uses the same pattern: <em lang=\"es\">haber</em> in the matching simple tense plus the past participle.";

    private static readonly GuideMood[] Moods =
    [
        new("indicativo", "Indicativo", "indicative",
            "The mood of facts: what happens, happened or will happen. Most of what you say and read is in the indicative.",
        [
            new("indicativo-presente", "presente", "present", false,
                "what is true now, habits and general truths, and often the near future. For an action going on right now, <em lang=\"es\">estar</em> + gerund is common (<em lang=\"es\">estoy comiendo</em>).",
                "stem + present endings.",
                Yo, Rows(["o", "as", "a", "amos", "áis", "an"], ["o", "es", "e", "emos", "éis", "en"], ["o", "es", "e", "imos", "ís", "en"]),
                [("Hablo español.", "I speak Spanish."),
                 ("Todas las mañanas tomo un café.", "Every morning I have a coffee."),
                 ("Mañana salgo para Madrid.", "I'm leaving for Madrid tomorrow."),
                 ("Estudio español desde hace dos años.", "I have been studying Spanish for two years.")]),
            new("indicativo-preterito-perfecto", "pretérito perfecto", "present perfect", true,
                "a past action connected to the present: today, this week, ever, never, already. In most of Spain it is also the normal past for things that happened today; in Latin America the <em lang=\"es\">indefinido</em> is often used instead.",
                $"present of <em lang=\"es\">haber</em> + past participle. {CompoundNote}",
                Yo, null,
                [("Hoy he comido paella.", "I've had paella today."),
                 ("¿Has estado alguna vez en México?", "Have you ever been to Mexico?"),
                 ("Todavía no hemos terminado.", "We haven't finished yet.")]),
            new("indicativo-preterito-indefinido", "pretérito indefinido", "preterite", false,
                "a completed action at a definite time in the past: yesterday, last year, in 1492. It tells what happened; the <em lang=\"es\">imperfecto</em> describes the background. Grammars also call it <em lang=\"es\">pretérito perfecto simple</em>.",
                "stem + preterite endings. Many common verbs have an irregular stem and unstressed endings (<em lang=\"es\">tener → tuve, tuvo; hacer → hice, hizo; decir → dije</em>), and <em lang=\"es\">ser</em> and <em lang=\"es\">ir</em> share their forms (<em lang=\"es\">fui, fue</em>).",
                Yo, Rows(["é", "aste", "ó", "amos", "asteis", "aron"], ["í", "iste", "ió", "imos", "isteis", "ieron"], ["í", "iste", "ió", "imos", "isteis", "ieron"]),
                [("Ayer fuimos a la playa.", "Yesterday we went to the beach."),
                 ("Colón llegó a América en 1492.", "Columbus reached America in 1492."),
                 ("¿Qué dijiste?", "What did you say?")]),
            new("indicativo-preterito-anterior", "pretérito anterior", "past anterior", true,
                "only in literary writing: something that happened just before another past event, after <em lang=\"es\">cuando, apenas, en cuanto</em>. Speech uses the <em lang=\"es\">indefinido</em> or the <em lang=\"es\">pluscuamperfecto</em>.",
                "preterite of <em lang=\"es\">haber</em> + past participle.",
                Yo, null,
                [("Apenas hubo terminado, se marchó.", "As soon as he had finished, he left.")]),
            new("indicativo-preterito-imperfecto", "pretérito imperfecto", "imperfect", false,
                "the past seen from the inside: what used to happen, what was going on, and descriptions of how things were. It sets the scene; the <em lang=\"es\">indefinido</em> tells what happened in it.",
                "stem + <em lang=\"es\">-aba</em> endings for <em lang=\"es\">-ar</em> verbs, <em lang=\"es\">-ía</em> endings for the others. Only three verbs are irregular: <em lang=\"es\">ser (era), ir (iba), ver (veía)</em>.",
                Yo, Rows(["aba", "abas", "aba", "ábamos", "abais", "aban"], ["ía", "ías", "ía", "íamos", "íais", "ían"], ["ía", "ías", "ía", "íamos", "íais", "ían"]),
                [("De niño jugaba al fútbol.", "As a child I used to play football."),
